<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yuan Shu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 199</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yuan Shu was a Chinese warlord of the late Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> and cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a>. Holding the Huainan region, he proclaimed himself emperor of a state called Zhong in 197, which made him an outcast among the other warlords. Defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-bu">Lü Bu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, he tried to join Yuan Shao in the north in 199 but was blocked and died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuan-shu.jpg" alt="Yuan Shu" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuan_Shu_Qing_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yuan Shu, courtesy name Gonglu, was born in 155 in Ruyang County, Runan Commandery, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>. His family had been prominent in the Han civil service for more than four generations, with members in high office since the first century CE. He was a son of the Minister of Works Yuan Feng. As a young man he gained a reputation for gallantry and liked to hunt with dogs and falcons. Nominated as Filial and Incorrupt, he became Intendant of Henan and then General of the Household Rapid as a Tiger.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the death of General-in-Chief He Jin on 22 September 189, Yuan Shu led the Imperial Tiger Guards against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> faction. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo">Dong Zhuo</a> seized the Han government he fled to Nanyang Commandery, which he took over after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-jian">Sun Jian</a> killed its grand administrator. He joined the campaign against Dong Zhuo led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a>, but when it broke up in 191 the two vied for northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, each forming alliances. Defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> and Yuan Shao in 193, including at the Battle of Fengqiu, he withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shouchun">Shouchun</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a> and built a powerful warlord state there. His rule in Nanyang had been despotic and his regime extravagant.</p><p>From 194 to early 197 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-ce">Sun Ce</a> and Wu Jing conquered much of Jiangdong on his behalf, but he fared worse in Xu Province against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-bu">Lü Bu</a>. In early 197 he declared himself emperor, citing omens, among them the characters of his given and courtesy names and his possession of the Imperial Seal, which had passed to him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-jian">Sun Jian</a>. The act made him a target of the other warlords, and many followers deserted, Sun Ce among them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After crushing defeats by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-bu">Lü Bu</a>, Yuan Shu tried to flee north to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a>. Yuan Shao sent his eldest son Yuan Tan to help, but Yuan Tan arrived too late, and Liu Bei blocked and turned back Yuan Shu's forces to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shouchun">Shouchun</a>. He died soon afterwards in 199, of starvation, being unable to swallow the coarse food his soldiers ate. His last request was for honey water, which his soldiers did not have. His son Yuan Yao fled to Lujiang, was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-ce">Sun Ce</a> and later worked as a Palace Gentleman in Eastern Wu; Yuan Yao's daughter married Sun Fen, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yuan Shu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Yuan Shu</b>, of the powerful Yuan family, controlled the Huai River region in the chaos after the fall of Dong Zhuo.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhongjia">Zhongjia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Shangshui County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Anhui, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.54,114.61&amp;year=189" aria-label="Shangshui County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Yuan Shu"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4616 4688h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4616 4688h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.54,114.61&amp;year=189">What was here in 189 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
